  "final_text": "**Short answer: no, not in any way the data can show.** The drop from 3.21 to 2.51 NCRs per 1,000 units that the VP of Sales cites mostly comes from the bigger denominator. September has five weekly buckets and August has four. In the same number of weeks, CRAFT's defect rate is about the same as August's, and the number of affected units went up.\n\n## Recomputed from the raw data\n\nBoth report figures check out. 23 NCRs ÷ 9,162 units = 2.51, and 24 ÷ 7,488 = 3.21. Sources: `ncr_records.csv` filtered to line = CRAFT by `ncr_date` month, and `demand_actuals.csv` filtered to CRAFT SKUs by `week_start` month. Both are monthly totals. The problem is what's being compared.\n\n| CRAFT | Aug (4 wks) | Sep as reported (5 wks) | Sep, first 4 wks (2–23 Sep) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Units shipped | 7,488 | 9,162 | 7,127 |\n| NCRs (count) | 24 | 23 | 22 |\n| NCRs per 1,000 units | **3.21** | **2.51** | **3.09** |\n| Units affected (`qty_affected`) | 2,324 | 2,832 | 2,757 |\n| Units affected per unit shipped | 31.0% | 30.9% | 38.7% |\n\n## Why the reported improvement doesn't hold up\n\n- **The extra week explains most of the change.** The fifth bucket starts 30 Sep and is FY24-Q3-W01, so it's really an October week. It adds 2,035 units but only 1 NCR, dated 30 Sep, which suggests the NCR log for that week is incomplete. Without that week the rate is 3.09, about 4% below August, and that rests on a single NCR difference (22 vs 24). The report's own §6 warns that month-over-month totals aren't like-for-like, but §5 uses them anyway.\n- **Counting affected units instead of NCRs gives the opposite result.** Affected units rose from 2,324 to 2,832 (+22%). Per unit shipped it's flat at about 31% across the full months, and worse at 38.7% if you compare the same four weeks. Per week it's 581 in August against 566 or 689 in September, depending on which weeks you count.\n- **Raw NCR count is flat** (24 → 23), and CRAFT still has the most NCRs of any line in both months.\n\n## Limits of these numbers\n\n- These are production NCRs, not customer returns (report §6).\n- \"Units affected\" is production quantity, while the denominator is shipped demand. They come from two different sources, so the ratio is only an indicator.\n- Two months is too short to call a trend in either direction.\n- No team definition for \"defect rate\" was available, so I'm showing both readings (NCR count and affected units) rather than picking one.\n\n**What this means for the decision:** CRAFT's quality hasn't clearly improved, so it doesn't support moving Q3 marketing money ahead of the rework. One condition would change that: NCRs per 1,000 units below about 2.5 **and** affected units per unit shipped below about 25%, sustained over at least four matched weeks. You'd want both measures because the NCR rate alone can drop just by merging NCRs or shifting the week window.",
